    <h2>Common Third-Trimester Symptoms</h2>

    <h3>Shortness of Breath</h3>
    <p>As the uterus expands, it pushes against the diaphragm, making it harder to take deep breaths. This usually improves when the baby "drops" (engages in the pelvis) in the weeks before birth.</p>

    <h3>Frequent Urination</h3>
    <p>The baby's head pressing on the bladder causes more frequent trips to the bathroom. This is normal but can be uncomfortable.</p>

    <h3>Heartburn and Indigestion</h3>
    <p>Pressure on the stomach continues to cause acid reflux. Eating smaller meals and avoiding lying down immediately after eating can help.</p>

    <h3>Swelling (Edema)</h3>
    <p>Mild swelling of the feet, ankles, and hands is normal in late pregnancy. Sudden or severe swelling, especially in the face or hands, should be reported to your clinician immediately.</p>

    <h3>Braxton Hicks Contractions</h3>
    <p>Practice contractions that are usually painless and irregular. They are the body's way of preparing for labor. True labor contractions become regular, stronger, and closer together over time.</p>

    <h3>Lightening (Baby Dropping)</h3>
    <p>The baby settles into the pelvis, usually 2-4 weeks before birth in first pregnancies. You may notice easier breathing but more pressure on the pelvis and bladder.</p>

    <h2>Third-Trimester Screening Tests</h2>

    <h3>Group B Strep (GBS) Screening</h3>
    <p>Group B strep is a common bacterium that often lives in the rectum or vagina. It is usually harmless, but it can pass to a baby during birth and occasionally cause a serious infection in newborns.</p>
    <p>Guidance differs by country. In the United States, ACOG and the CDC recommend screening every pregnant woman with a swab at 36 to 37 weeks, and antibiotics during labor for those who test positive. The NHS does not offer routine testing; testing is offered to those with specific risk factors instead.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: CDC, Screening for Group B Strep Bacteria, https://www.cdc.gov/group-b-strep/testing/index.html; NHS, Group B strep, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/group-b-strep/</em></p>

    <h3>Non-Stress Test (NST)</h3>
    <p>Monitors the baby's heart rate in response to movement. Usually performed if you have a high-risk pregnancy, decreased fetal movement, or are past your due date.</p>

    <h3>Biophysical Profile (BPP)</h3>
    <p>An ultrasound that checks five factors: fetal movement, muscle tone, breathing, amniotic fluid volume, and heart rate.</p>

    <h2>Your Baby's Movements in the Third Trimester</h2>

    <p>You should feel your baby move right up to and during labour. There is no set number of movements you should feel each day, and you do not need to count kicks. Get to know your baby's usual pattern of movements from day to day.</p>

    <p>Call your midwife or maternity unit immediately if your baby is moving less than usual, if you cannot feel your baby moving anymore, or if there is a change to the usual pattern. Do not wait until the next day, and do not use a home doppler to check the heartbeat yourself.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Your baby's movements, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/</em></p>

    <h2>Signs of Preterm Labour</h2>

    <p>Premature labour is labour that happens before the 37th week of pregnancy. About 8 out of 100 babies are born prematurely.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>regular contractions or tightenings</li>
      <li>period-type pains</li>
      <li>a gush or trickle of fluid from your vagina, which could be your waters breaking</li>
      <li>backache that is not usual for you</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Call your midwife or maternity unit if you are less than 37 weeks pregnant and you have any of these. Contact your clinician immediately if they appear.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Premature labour and birth, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/labour-and-birth/premature-labour-and-birth/</em></p>

