    <h2>Preparing for Birth</h2>

    <h3>Birth Plan</h3>
    <p>A birth plan outlines your preferences for labor and delivery, including pain management, positions for pushing, and who you want in the room. It is a communication tool, not a rigid contract.</p>
    <p>A birth plan is a way of letting your midwife, nurses and doctors know what you want to happen during your labour.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Where do you want to give birth: at home, in a midwifery unit or in hospital?</li>
      <li>Who do you want with you, and what birthing equipment or facilities would you like?</li>
      <li>What are your pain relief preferences, and do you want skin-to-skin contact before the cord is cut?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Giving birth does not always go perfectly to plan, and things may change at the last minute.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, What to include in your birth plan, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/preparing-for-labour-and-birth/what-to-include-in-your-birth-plan/</em></p>

    <h3>Hospital Bag</h3>
    <p>Pack a bag by week 36 with essentials for you, your partner, and the baby. Include comfortable clothes, toiletries, phone charger, snacks, and a going-home outfit for the baby.</p>

    <h3>Signs of Labor</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Regular contractions that get stronger and closer together</li>
      <li>Water breaking (rupture of membranes)</li>
      <li>Bloody show (mucus plug)</li>
      <li>Low back pain or cramping</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Signs that labour might be starting include contractions or tightenings, a show when the plug of mucus from your cervix comes away, backache, and your waters breaking. Call your midwife or maternity unit if you are having regular contractions every 5 minutes or more often. Call urgently if your waters break or your baby is moving less than usual.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Signs that labour has begun, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/labour-and-birth/signs-that-labour-has-begun/</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider immediately if you experience:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Heavy vaginal bleeding</li>
      <li>Severe headache that does not resolve</li>
      <li>Visual changes (blurring, flashing lights, floaters)</li>
      <li>Sudden swelling of hands, face, or eyes</li>
      <li>Decreased fetal movement</li>
      <li>Signs of labor before 37 weeks</li>
      <li>Fever over 100.4°F (38°C)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Term Definitions</h2>

    <p>These categories describe how far along a pregnancy is. A pregnancy is measured in weeks and days, so 37 0/7 means 37 weeks and 0 days.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Classification</th>
          <th>Weeks</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Preterm</td>
          <td>Before 37 0/7</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Early term</td>
          <td>37 0/7 – 38 6/7</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Full term</td>
          <td>39 0/7 – 40 6/7</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Late term</td>
          <td>41 0/7 – 41 6/7</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Postterm</td>
          <td>42 0/7 and beyond</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion, Definition of Term Pregnancy.</em></p>

    <p>Your midwife refers to these categories when discussing care in late pregnancy. If you are still pregnant after 40 weeks, your choices will be discussed at the 38 and 40 week appointments.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Antenatal (pregnancy) care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

